$DIR/main.p:17:7: p1 holds a view of a1 (a1.Alloc(8, 8), line 15), which was dropped by a1.Drop() at line 16, so p1 can no longer be used here
    p1 looks at memory that a1 owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    a1.Alloc(8, 8) at line 15. A view of an owner held in a local variable may
    be used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At
    line 16 a1 was dropped by a1.Drop(), so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p1 before line 16, or drop a1 only after the last use of p1
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:22:6: p2 holds a view of a2 (a2.Alloc(16, 8), line 20), which was moved into takeArena (its parameter takes a pluto/arena.Chunked by value) at line 21, so p2 can no longer be used here
    p2 looks at memory that a2 owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    a2.Alloc(16, 8) at line 20. A view of an owner held in a local variable may
    be used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At
    line 21 a2 was moved into takeArena (its parameter takes a
    pluto/arena.Chunked by value), so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p2 before line 21, or move a2 only after the last use of p2
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
